1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Every sunny day, ultraviolet light from the Sun

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,160
damages the DNA in your skin cells, many

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,400
times over in each exposed cell.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,600
Yet almost all of that damage never becomes

5
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:12,640
a mutation.

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,440
Your cells fix it.

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,760
So what exactly does UV do to DNA?

8
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:20,960
What do X-rays and some chemicals do differently?

9
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,560
And why doesn't damage always become a mutation?

10
00:00:25,767 --> 00:00:28,246
Start with two words that sound alike.

11
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:31,126
A mutagen is anything that raises the chance

12
00:00:31,167 --> 00:00:34,246
of a change in DNA, like UV light,

13
00:00:34,327 --> 00:00:36,486
X-rays or certain chemicals.

14
00:00:36,807 --> 00:00:39,766
A mutation is the change itself, a different

15
00:00:39,927 --> 00:00:41,286
base sequence.

16
00:00:41,407 --> 00:00:43,686
So UV isn't a mutation.

17
00:00:43,727 --> 00:00:45,206
It's a mutagen.

18
00:00:45,327 --> 00:00:48,486
Mutations also happen without any mutagen, from rare

19
00:00:48,567 --> 00:00:50,006
copying mistakes.

20
00:00:50,047 --> 00:00:51,926
A mutagen raises that rate.

21
00:00:53,134 --> 00:00:55,933
Here's how a damaged base becomes a mutation.

22
00:00:56,494 --> 00:00:59,293
A mutagen damages one spot on the DNA.

23
00:00:59,774 --> 00:01:02,573
Repair enzymes usually find the damage and fix

24
00:01:02,654 --> 00:01:05,213
it, using the other strand as a guide.

25
00:01:05,534 --> 00:01:08,173
But if the cell copies its DNA before

26
00:01:08,214 --> 00:01:10,973
the repair happens, the copying enzyme can put

27
00:01:11,014 --> 00:01:13,293
the wrong base opposite the damage.

28
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:16,013
When that new strand is copied again, the

29
00:01:16,094 --> 00:01:18,333
wrong base gets its own matching partner.

30
00:01:18,974 --> 00:01:21,213
Now the change is in both strands.

31
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:23,533
It's permanent, a mutation.

32
00:01:24,734 --> 00:01:27,186
That's why exposure is a risk, not a

33
00:01:27,214 --> 00:01:28,213
guarantee.

34
00:01:28,494 --> 00:01:31,773
More exposure means more damage, so more chances

35
00:01:31,894 --> 00:01:34,173
that some of it is copied before repair,

36
00:01:34,294 --> 00:01:35,653
or repaired wrongly.

37
00:01:35,934 --> 00:01:38,253
A stronger source, or a longer time in

38
00:01:38,334 --> 00:01:41,213
it, damages more spots at once, and repair

39
00:01:41,294 --> 00:01:42,253
can fall behind.

40
00:01:42,654 --> 00:01:45,213
And a mutagen can't choose where it hits,

41
00:01:45,294 --> 00:01:47,533
so the change could land in any gene,

42
00:01:47,654 --> 00:01:49,213
or in no gene at all.

43
00:01:50,434 --> 00:01:54,593
The first group of mutagens is electromagnetic radiation.

44
00:01:54,674 --> 00:01:57,873
UV light, X-rays and gamma rays are all

45
00:01:57,954 --> 00:02:00,513
part of the same spectrum as visible light,

46
00:02:00,594 --> 00:02:02,593
but they carry more energy.

47
00:02:02,674 --> 00:02:05,313
The UV in sunlight can't knock electrons off

48
00:02:05,394 --> 00:02:08,593
atoms, but it can join two neighbouring thymine

49
00:02:08,674 --> 00:02:11,633
bases on the same strand, forming a thymine

50
00:02:11,714 --> 00:02:12,593
dimer.

51
00:02:12,714 --> 00:02:15,473
That kink distorts the DNA, so when it's

52
00:02:15,554 --> 00:02:17,953
copied, the wrong base can go in.

53
00:02:18,034 --> 00:02:20,353
UV doesn't get far into the body, so

54
00:02:20,434 --> 00:02:22,513
it mainly damages skin cells.

55
00:02:23,734 --> 00:02:26,773
X-rays and gamma rays carry much more energy.

56
00:02:27,174 --> 00:02:30,133
They're ionising, which means they can knock electrons

57
00:02:30,214 --> 00:02:31,253
off atoms.

58
00:02:31,574 --> 00:02:34,773
That can snap the DNA backbone directly, or

59
00:02:34,934 --> 00:02:38,133
split water molecules into reactive fragments called free

60
00:02:38,214 --> 00:02:40,613
radicals, which then attack DNA.

61
00:02:41,254 --> 00:02:43,013
Sometimes both strands break.

62
00:02:43,574 --> 00:02:45,413
If the cell joins the broken ends back

63
00:02:45,494 --> 00:02:47,973
wrongly, a whole piece can be lost or

64
00:02:48,134 --> 00:02:48,613
swapped.

65
00:02:49,414 --> 00:02:51,893
These rays pass deep into the body, so

66
00:02:51,974 --> 00:02:54,453
any cell can be hit, including cells that

67
00:02:54,534 --> 00:02:56,053
make eggs or sperm.

68
00:02:57,267 --> 00:03:00,226
The second group is chemical mutagens, and they

69
00:03:00,347 --> 00:03:02,386
work in three main ways.

70
00:03:02,547 --> 00:03:04,626
Some look so much like a normal base

71
00:03:04,707 --> 00:03:07,346
that the cell builds them into new DNA,

72
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:10,306
but later they pair with the wrong partner.

73
00:03:10,387 --> 00:03:12,386
That's a base analogue.

74
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:15,586
Some, like chemicals in tobacco smoke, attach to

75
00:03:15,667 --> 00:03:18,146
a base and change how it pairs.

76
00:03:18,227 --> 00:03:21,266
And some slide in between neighbouring base pairs,

77
00:03:21,347 --> 00:03:23,626
so the copying enzyme adds or skips a

78
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:26,386
base, which shifts every codon after it.

79
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,280
The third group is naturally occurring mutagens, and

80
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:33,840
natural doesn't mean harmless.

81
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:37,760
Background radiation, from rocks and soil, radon gas

82
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:40,800
and space, is ionising, so it can break

83
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,120
DNA strands, just like X-rays.

84
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,000
Aflatoxin, made by a mould that grows on

85
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:49,040
damp peanuts and grain, is a natural chemical

86
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,000
that, once the liver processes it, attaches to

87
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:52,640
bases.

88
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:56,080
And some viruses insert their own DNA into

89
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:56,560
ours.

90
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:58,960
If it lands inside a gene, or the

91
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:01,360
region that switches it on, that gene can

92
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,760
be broken, or switched on when it shouldn't

93
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:04,240
be.

94
00:04:05,467 --> 00:04:08,346
So to explain any mutagen, ask what it

95
00:04:08,507 --> 00:04:10,186
physically does to DNA.

96
00:04:10,827 --> 00:04:13,226
UV joins neighbouring bases.

97
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:16,106
Ionising radiation breaks strands.

98
00:04:16,347 --> 00:04:19,146
Chemicals swap in for bases, change them, or

99
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:20,346
squeeze between them.

100
00:04:20,827 --> 00:04:23,466
Some viruses insert new DNA.

101
00:04:23,787 --> 00:04:25,186
Then the last step.

102
00:04:25,467 --> 00:04:28,826
Damaged bases become mutations when DNA is copied

103
00:04:28,907 --> 00:04:29,946
before repair.

104
00:04:30,427 --> 00:04:33,386
A break joined back wrongly, or inserted viral

105
00:04:33,467 --> 00:04:36,346
DNA, can also make a lasting change.

106
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:38,686
Two things to keep.

107
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:42,046
A mutagen is the cause, and a mutation

108
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:43,086
is the change.

109
00:04:43,727 --> 00:04:44,766
And for radiation:

110
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:47,646
UV kinks, X-rays snap.

111
00:04:48,207 --> 00:04:50,846
UV can't knock electrons off, so it bonds

112
00:04:50,927 --> 00:04:54,686
neighbouring bases, while X-rays are ionising, so they

113
00:04:54,807 --> 00:04:55,886
break the strands.

114
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:58,540
Two exam questions now.

115
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:03,340
First: Ultraviolet radiation and X-rays are both forms

116
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:05,260
of electromagnetic radiation.

117
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:08,620
Compare how each increases the risk of mutation

118
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,500
in human cells, and which cells each is

119
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:12,540
likely to affect.

120
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:14,380
Pause here to answer.

121
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:28,080
Both damage DNA, but repair fixes most of

122
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:30,560
it, so neither guarantees a mutation.

123
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:31,880
They differ.

124
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,240
UV isn't ionising.

125
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:37,600
It joins neighbouring thymines, kinking the DNA, so

126
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,080
wrong bases go in when it's copied, mostly

127
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:41,520
in skin cells.

128
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,440
X-rays are ionising, so they break DNA strands,

129
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:48,240
and wrongly rejoined breaks can lose or swap

130
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:50,640
pieces, even in deep cells.

131
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:54,000
Saying UV and X-rays damage DNA in exactly

132
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:56,560
the same way is the classic mistake.

133
00:05:57,767 --> 00:05:58,326
Second.

134
00:05:58,807 --> 00:06:01,286
A student spread equal volumes of the same

135
00:06:01,367 --> 00:06:04,646
yeast suspension onto agar plates, exposed them to

136
00:06:04,727 --> 00:06:06,966
UV light for different times at a fixed

137
00:06:07,247 --> 00:06:09,126
distance, then incubated them.

138
00:06:09,847 --> 00:06:12,086
The table shows the mean number of colonies

139
00:06:12,167 --> 00:06:13,806
at each exposure time.

140
00:06:14,167 --> 00:06:17,046
Describe the relationship between exposure time and the

141
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:18,246
number of colonies.

142
00:06:18,887 --> 00:06:22,646
Then assess the student's conclusion that UV increased

143
00:06:22,687 --> 00:06:24,406
the mutation rate in the yeast.

144
00:06:25,047 --> 00:06:26,406
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:40,033
As exposure time increases, the number of colonies

146
00:06:40,114 --> 00:06:42,913
falls, from a hundred and forty-two with no

147
00:06:43,074 --> 00:06:46,273
UV to just five after two minutes.

148
00:06:46,594 --> 00:06:48,513
But the conclusion goes too far.

149
00:06:48,994 --> 00:06:51,553
A colony count shows how many cells survived

150
00:06:51,634 --> 00:06:54,273
and divided, not how many mutated.

151
00:06:54,834 --> 00:06:57,553
To measure mutation, you'd need to count surviving

152
00:06:57,634 --> 00:06:59,473
colonies with a changed trait.

153
00:07:00,114 --> 00:07:02,993
Treating fewer colonies as proof of more mutations

154
00:07:03,074 --> 00:07:04,593
is the classic mistake.

155
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,680
So to explain how a mutagen works, name

156
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,080
its group, say what it physically does to

157
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:14,600
DNA, and finish with how the change lasts:

158
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:17,640
damage copied before repair, a break joined back

159
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:21,000
wrongly, or DNA inserted by a virus.

160
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:24,200
Next, you'll see what one changed base can

161
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:25,480
do to a protein.

162
00:07:26,700 --> 00:07:28,140
Here are the key notes.

163
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:30,300
Pause the video if you need more time

164
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:31,180
to copy them.
